  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 259 AM CST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nThe timing of the upper-level wave and surface low/cold front \ncontinues to gradually slow down among the available guidance \ncompared to recent runs, but confidence is increasing that rain \nalong and ahead of the cold front holds off until after midnight. At \nthat point, there is some potential for overnight showers as the low-\nlevel jet intensifies across the Mid-Mississippi Valley. A closer \nanalysis of the ensemble guidance members suggests continued \nvariance in the trough's structure, which will impact the track of \nthe surface low itself. That said, a majority of this guidance now \nsupports a few closed circulations embedded within the larger \ntrough: one originating from the polar jet and remaining to our \nnorth and the other forming over the central Plains. \n\nBoth of these features and their surface reflections will work in \nconcert to bring rain along the cold front and gusty 15-30mph winds \nthrough the day on Tuesday. The trough's forward progress is still \nexpected to stall through the day, weakening any height falls across \nour region. Precipitable water values near climatological maxima \nwill still be available to the front to promote precipitation along \nthe front. That said, the warm air aloft evident in model soundings \nand meager mixed-layer CAPE (as much as 250 J/kg in the most bullish \nguidance) won't bode well for robust, deep convection. Even in this \nworst-case scenario, it would be difficult for strong thunderstorms \nto develop. One feature that may assist in large-scale ascent is the \npresence of some favorable jet dynamics during the afternoon, but \ninstability is still quite meager overall. For the reasons outlined \nabove, we are choosing not to message the SPC Day 3 Marginal (Level \n1) Risk for severe weather across southeast Missouri on Tuesday \nafternoon. If instability increases and height falls become more \nsubstantial among the guidance, the threat would certainly become \nmore probable.\n\nRain will continue in rounds along and ahead of the front through \nlate Wednesday, but the trough becomes more established across the \ncentral CONUS and stalls. Several impulses will likely propagate \nthroughout the cyclonic flow aloft, each of which would threaten \nadditional showers through the rest of the work week. While our rain \nchances in the forecast, which is largely based on the NBM, are \nfairly low, that is more a function of where the rain falls, not if. \nAs such, I expect those values to climb a little bit as we approach \nthe end of the week. Still, we will begin to see more dry time once \nthe front passes to our east. We'll also enjoy cooler and slightly-\ndrier conditions through the duration of the week.",
